<?xml version="1.0"?>
<tt xmlns:vt="http://namespace.itunes.apple.com/itt/ttml-extension#vertical" xmlns:ttp="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#parameter" xmlns:ittp="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml/profile/imsc1#parameter" xmlns:tt_feature="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml/feature/" xmlns:ebutts="urn:ebu:tt:style" xmlns:tts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#styling" xmlns:tt_extension="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml/extension/" xmlns:tt_profile="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml/profile/" xmlns:ttm="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml#metadata" xmlns:ry="http://namespace.itunes.apple.com/itt/ttml-extension#ruby" xmlns:itts="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml/profile/imsc1#styling" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml" xml:lang="en" ttp:dropMode="nonDrop" ttp:frameRate="25" ttp:frameRateMultiplier="1 1" ttp:timeBase="smpte" xmlns:tt="http://www.w3.org/ns/ttml" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <head>
    <styling>
      <style xml:id="normal" tts:color="white" tts:fontFamily="sansSerif" tts:fontSize="100%" tts:fontStyle="normal" tts:fontWeight="normal"/>
    </styling>
    <layout>
      <region xml:id="bottom" tts:displayAlign="after" tts:extent="100% 15%" tts:origin="0% 85%" tts:writingMode="lrtb"/>
    </layout>
  </head>
  <body tts:color="white" region="bottom" style="normal">
    <div>
      <p begin="00:00:00:00" end="00:00:02:00" region="bottom">Welcome to the C++ Club.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:02:00" end="00:00:04:00" region="bottom">This is Meeting 173.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:06:00" end="00:00:09:00" region="bottom">Right, first of all, I wanted to mention one of</p>
      <p begin="00:00:09:00" end="00:00:12:00" region="bottom"> us who we lost.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:13:00" end="00:00:17:00" region="bottom">Rainer Grimm sadly passed away in October last year.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:17:00" end="00:00:20:00" region="bottom">I didn't know him personally, but he</p>
      <p begin="00:00:20:00" end="00:00:23:00" region="bottom"> seemed to have been a great guy and an</p>
      <p begin="00:00:23:00" end="00:00:26:00" region="bottom"> excellent C++ educator, and it's a sad loss.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:29:00" end="00:00:32:00" region="bottom">I've met him a couple of times at different conferences,</p>
      <p begin="00:00:32:00" end="00:00:35:00" region="bottom"> and you're absolutely right in your assessment.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:35:00" end="00:00:37:00" region="bottom">It's so unfortunate.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:37:00" end="00:00:40:00" region="bottom">Maybe, I may give you</p>
      <p begin="00:00:40:00" end="00:00:44:00" region="bottom"> the information that</p>
      <p begin="00:00:45:00" end="00:00:48:00" region="bottom"> his son is trying to keep</p>
      <p begin="00:00:48:00" end="00:00:50:00" region="bottom"> on with his blog here.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:50:00" end="00:00:54:00" region="bottom">And I might appear</p>
      <p begin="00:00:54:00" end="00:00:56:00" region="bottom"> there sometime in the future.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:58:00" end="00:00:59:00" region="bottom">That's, that's good to hear.</p>
      <p begin="00:00:59:00" end="00:01:00:00" region="bottom">That's good news.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:00:00" end="00:01:01:00" region="bottom">Thank you.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:01:00" end="00:01:05:00" region="bottom">Hopefully, his life</p>
      <p begin="00:01:05:00" end="00:01:07:00" region="bottom"> work continues this way.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:10:00" end="00:01:13:00" region="bottom">Next item is</p>
      <p begin="00:01:13:00" end="00:01:15:00" region="bottom"> a bit of a feedback reaction.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:16:00" end="00:01:19:00" region="bottom">Last time, which was a very long, long time</p>
      <p begin="00:01:19:00" end="00:01:22:00" region="bottom"> ago, I mistakenly said</p>
      <p begin="00:01:22:00" end="00:01:25:00" region="bottom"> that CMake was a product of</p>
      <p begin="00:01:25:00" end="00:01:28:00" region="bottom"> the company called KDAB.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:28:00" end="00:01:29:00" region="bottom">Obviously that was wrong.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:30:00" end="00:01:32:00" region="bottom">I am correcting my record.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:32:00" end="00:01:34:00" region="bottom">It's, of course, Kitware.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:34:00" end="00:01:36:00" region="bottom">And we'll talk about CMake later.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:36:00" end="00:01:37:00" region="bottom">Other news.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:38:00" end="00:01:39:00" region="bottom">I have a blog now.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:39:00" end="00:01:40:00" region="bottom">Yay!</p>
      <p begin="00:01:41:00" end="00:01:42:00" region="bottom">It's pretty much empty.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:43:00" end="00:01:45:00" region="bottom">Except for one record.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:45:00" end="00:01:48:00" region="bottom">Sort of an explanation why it took me so</p>
      <p begin="00:01:48:00" end="00:01:49:00" region="bottom"> long to restart the meetings.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:50:00" end="00:01:54:00" region="bottom">I've been on a very intense</p>
      <p begin="00:01:54:00" end="00:01:57:00" region="bottom"> project since the last meeting.</p>
      <p begin="00:01:57:00" end="00:01:59:00" region="bottom">The project lasted for about a year and a half.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:00:00" end="00:02:03:00" region="bottom">It was a really, really interesting project.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:03:00" end="00:02:06:00" region="bottom">Now that it's over, I wanted to</p>
      <p begin="00:02:06:00" end="00:02:08:00" region="bottom"> mention what I was doing there.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:08:00" end="00:02:12:00" region="bottom">I was brought in to implement</p>
      <p begin="00:02:12:00" end="00:02:15:00" region="bottom"> a particular piece of infrastructure,</p>
      <p begin="00:02:15:00" end="00:02:18:00" region="bottom"> expose an internal system to</p>
      <p begin="00:02:18:00" end="00:02:22:00" region="bottom"> clients using a web service.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:22:00" end="00:02:23:00" region="bottom">And while I was doing that,</p>
      <p begin="00:02:24:00" end="00:02:27:00" region="bottom">naturally, I tried to optimise</p>
      <p begin="00:02:27:00" end="00:02:30:00" region="bottom"> the build system, as one does,</p>
      <p begin="00:02:30:00" end="00:02:33:00" region="bottom"> made it quicker than before.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:33:00" end="00:02:36:00" region="bottom">That particular code base built in 7 minutes, before I</p>
      <p begin="00:02:37:00" end="00:02:41:00" region="bottom"> started with it, and after that,</p>
      <p begin="00:02:41:00" end="00:02:44:00" region="bottom"> it was less than half a minute.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:44:00" end="00:02:47:00" region="bottom">Obviously, I cheated</p>
      <p begin="00:02:47:00" end="00:02:49:00" region="bottom"> a little bit with ccache.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:49:00" end="00:02:51:00" region="bottom">Anything to make the build quicker.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:51:00" end="00:02:53:00" region="bottom">And then I was extended to do</p>
      <p begin="00:02:54:00" end="00:02:57:00" region="bottom"> the same build improvements in another code base.</p>
      <p begin="00:02:57:00" end="00:03:01:00" region="bottom">Did you use any particular tools to analyse the build times?</p>
      <p begin="00:03:03:00" end="00:03:04:00" region="bottom">Later on</p>
      <p begin="00:03:04:00" end="00:03:07:00" region="bottom">I introduced ClangBuildAnalyzer.</p>
      <p begin="00:03:07:00" end="00:03:10:00" region="bottom">And that helped pinpoint</p>
      <p begin="00:03:10:00" end="00:03:13:00" region="bottom"> parts of the code that the other</p>
      <p begin="00:03:13:00" end="00:03:15:00" region="bottom"> developers then looked at.</p>
      <p begin="00:03:16:00" end="00:03:19:00" region="bottom">But my main objective,</p>
      <p begin="00:03:19:00" end="00:03:22:00" region="bottom"> apart from the actual main one, was</p>
      <p begin="00:03:23:00" end="00:03:26:00" region="bottom"> I wanted to introduce static checking,</p>
      <p begin="00:03:26:00" end="00:03:29:00" region="bottom"> because they only had pretty</p>
      <p begin="00:03:29:00" end="00:03:32:00" region="bottom"> much a single build using CMake, and</p>
      <p begin="00:03:32:00" end="00:03:36:00" region="bottom"> that was either release or debug, depending</p>
      <p begin="00:03:36:00" end="00:03:39:00" region="bottom"> on what configuration command you ran</p>
      <p begin="00:03:39:00" end="00:03:42:00" region="bottom"> last, everything built into the</p>
      <p begin="00:03:42:00" end="00:03:45:00" region="bottom"> same build directory, which was inconvenient, and also</p>
      <p begin="00:03:45:00" end="00:03:46:00" region="bottom"> in the other code base</p>
      <p begin="00:03:47:00" end="00:03:51:00" region="bottom">they did something I never encountered</p>
      <p begin="00:03:51:00" end="00:03:55:00" region="bottom"> before, which was they built their test binaries</p>
      <p begin="00:03:55:00" end="00:03:57:00" region="bottom"> into the source tree.</p>
      <p begin="00:03:58:00" end="00:03:59:00" region="bottom">And that was not ideal.</p>
      <p begin="00:03:59:00" end="00:04:00:00" region="bottom">That doesn't sound right?</p>
      <p begin="00:04:00:00" end="00:04:04:00" region="bottom">Yeah, it was not ideal.</p>
      <p begin="00:04:04:00" end="00:04:07:00" region="bottom">My goal was, first of all, to enable</p>
      <p begin="00:04:07:00" end="00:04:10:00" region="bottom"> different build types in such a way that each</p>
      <p begin="00:04:10:00" end="00:04:14:00" region="bottom"> of them built into its own build directory (build tree).</p>
      <p begin="00:04:15:00" end="00:04:18:00" region="bottom">My idea was that if I had that,</p>
      <p begin="00:04:18:00" end="00:04:21:00" region="bottom"> I could then set up a</p>
      <p begin="00:04:21:00" end="00:04:24:00" region="bottom"> continuous build in such a way that all those</p>
      <p begin="00:04:24:00" end="00:04:27:00" region="bottom"> builds could be done in parallel, because they would</p>
      <p begin="00:04:27:00" end="00:04:30:00" region="bottom"> not be interfering with each other.</p>
      <p begin="00:04:30:00" end="00:04:33:00" region="bottom">But in the second code base, that wouldn't</p>
      <p begin="00:04:33:00" end="00:04:36:00" region="bottom"> be possible because the test binaries would</p>
      <p begin="00:04:37:00" end="00:04:39:00" region="bottom"> overwrite each other.</p>
      <p begin="00:04:39:00" end="00:04:42:00" region="bottom">There would be race conditions in the file system, which I didn't want.</p>
      <p begin="00:04:42:00" end="00:04:45:00" region="bottom">I can see why you're experimenting with Bazel.</p>
      <p begin="00:04:47:00" end="00:04:49:00" region="bottom">Did you have to then go back to CMake, I see here.</p>
      <p begin="00:04:49:00" end="00:04:52:00" region="bottom">They only use CMake, as often</p>
      <p begin="00:04:53:00" end="00:04:55:00" region="bottom"> happens with CMake.</p>
      <p begin="00:04:55:00" end="00:04:58:00" region="bottom">You just Google a working</p>
      <p begin="00:04:58:00" end="00:05:01:00" region="bottom"> configuration, then you cut and paste lots of</p>
      <p begin="00:05:01:00" end="00:05:04:00" region="bottom"> stuff, and you then don't touch it because</p>
      <p begin="00:05:04:00" end="00:05:06:00" region="bottom"> it works. Or you think it works.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:06:00" end="00:05:08:00" region="bottom">You hope not to touch it.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:08:00" end="00:05:09:00" region="bottom">You hope it works.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:09:00" end="00:05:12:00" region="bottom">And slowly, over time, it grows</p>
      <p begin="00:05:12:00" end="00:05:15:00" region="bottom"> various appendages and strange</p>
      <p begin="00:05:15:00" end="00:05:17:00" region="bottom"> build commands that you have to run.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:17:00" end="00:05:20:00" region="bottom">So unpicking that took quite a long time,</p>
      <p begin="00:05:21:00" end="00:05:25:00" region="bottom"> but in the end, I had builds</p>
      <p begin="00:05:25:00" end="00:05:28:00" region="bottom"> that used CMake profiles,</p>
      <p begin="00:05:29:00" end="00:05:32:00" region="bottom"> which, frankly, saved me.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:32:00" end="00:05:34:00" region="bottom">It was such a great life</p>
      <p begin="00:05:34:00" end="00:05:38:00" region="bottom"> quality improvement for anyone who works with CMake.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:38:00" end="00:05:41:00" region="bottom">I came up with a scheme where the profile</p>
      <p begin="00:05:42:00" end="00:05:47:00" region="bottom"> name expressed the build flavour, so to speak.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:47:00" end="00:05:51:00" region="bottom">For example, I had a build called gcc_deb</p>
      <p begin="00:05:51:00" end="00:05:55:00" region="bottom">and gcc_rel.</p>
      <p begin="00:05:56:00" end="00:05:59:00" region="bottom">And then I also introduced Clang</p>
      <p begin="00:05:59:00" end="00:06:02:00" region="bottom"> compiler. The production was GCC,</p>
      <p begin="00:06:02:00" end="00:06:06:00" region="bottom"> but I could run clang building parallel, and that</p>
      <p begin="00:06:06:00" end="00:06:09:00" region="bottom"> enabled me to also use all the clang tools,</p>
      <p begin="00:06:09:00" end="00:06:12:00" region="bottom"> including clang-tidy, all the clang sanitizers</p>
      <p begin="00:06:12:00" end="00:06:15:00" region="bottom"> alongside GCC sanitizers.</p>
      <p begin="00:06:15:00" end="00:06:18:00" region="bottom">I was lucky that they stayed on the pretty</p>
      <p begin="00:06:18:00" end="00:06:21:00" region="bottom"> much the latest compilers, so I think by</p>
      <p begin="00:06:22:00" end="00:06:25:00" region="bottom"> the end of it, we used GCC 14</p>
      <p begin="00:06:25:00" end="00:06:28:00" region="bottom"> and Clang 19.</p>
      <p begin="00:06:28:00" end="00:06:32:00" region="bottom">Well, not the cutting edge, but still pretty new.</p>
      <p begin="00:06:32:00" end="00:06:36:00" region="bottom">Better than the average of the industry.</p>
      <p begin="00:06:36:00" end="00:06:38:00" region="bottom">Yeah, definitely better than many.</p>
      <p begin="00:06:38:00" end="00:06:42:00" region="bottom">So yeah, by the end of that exercise,</p>
      <p begin="00:06:42:00" end="00:06:45:00" region="bottom"> I had those builds running in</p>
      <p begin="00:06:45:00" end="00:06:48:00" region="bottom"> parallel, those included debug,</p>
      <p begin="00:06:48:00" end="00:06:51:00" region="bottom"> release, all</p>
      <p begin="00:06:51:00" end="00:06:54:00" region="bottom"> the sanitizers: address, undefined</p>
      <p begin="00:06:55:00" end="00:06:58:00" region="bottom"> behavior and thread. Setting up memory sanitizer</p>
      <p begin="00:06:58:00" end="00:07:00:00" region="bottom"> is exercise in frustration,</p>
      <p begin="00:07:00:00" end="00:07:01:00" region="bottom">So I didn't do that.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:01:00" end="00:07:03:00" region="bottom">Did you have Valgrind on the side?</p>
      <p begin="00:07:03:00" end="00:07:06:00" region="bottom">Another build was release with debug</p>
      <p begin="00:07:06:00" end="00:07:07:00" region="bottom"> information for Valgrind.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:08:00" end="00:07:11:00" region="bottom">Everything was running on Jenkins.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:11:00" end="00:07:14:00" region="bottom">And yeah, the entire build set took</p>
      <p begin="00:07:14:00" end="00:07:16:00" region="bottom"> about an hour.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:16:00" end="00:07:17:00" region="bottom">That's pretty good.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:17:00" end="00:07:21:00" region="bottom">Yeah, and I scheduled it once a day</p>
      <p begin="00:07:21:00" end="00:07:24:00" region="bottom">at night so that it didn't interfere</p>
      <p begin="00:07:24:00" end="00:07:25:00" region="bottom"> with any other builds.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:26:00" end="00:07:28:00" region="bottom">The 1st code base was</p>
      <p begin="00:07:28:00" end="00:07:29:00" region="bottom">1.5 years old,</p>
      <p begin="00:07:29:00" end="00:07:32:00" region="bottom">The other one was more than 10 or even maybe</p>
      <p begin="00:07:32:00" end="00:07:33:00" region="bottom"> 15 years old.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:33:00" end="00:07:36:00" region="bottom">C++23 was enabled in both.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:36:00" end="00:07:39:00" region="bottom">And it's not like everyone suddenly started</p>
      <p begin="00:07:40:00" end="00:07:43:00" region="bottom"> using those C++23 features, but if</p>
      <p begin="00:07:43:00" end="00:07:47:00" region="bottom"> anyone wanted to, they were there. Like string::contains.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:47:00" end="00:07:49:00" region="bottom">Oh my god, imagine that.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:49:00" end="00:07:53:00" region="bottom">Most new features were in use, especially,</p>
      <p begin="00:07:53:00" end="00:07:54:00" region="bottom"> especially concepts.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:54:00" end="00:07:55:00" region="bottom">That was nice.</p>
      <p begin="00:07:55:00" end="00:07:58:00" region="bottom">That was my 1st time dealing with using concepts</p>
      <p begin="00:07:58:00" end="00:08:01:00" region="bottom"> and I really, really liked it.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:01:00" end="00:08:04:00" region="bottom">There was quite a lot of template code.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:04:00" end="00:08:07:00" region="bottom">I decided after some Googling to</p>
      <p begin="00:08:07:00" end="00:08:12:00" region="bottom"> use Drogon framework for creating a web service.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:12:00" end="00:08:13:00" region="bottom">That was really nice.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:13:00" end="00:08:16:00" region="bottom">And I combined it with some templated code</p>
      <p begin="00:08:17:00" end="00:08:20:00" region="bottom"> to make it easy to add new endpoints.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:20:00" end="00:08:23:00" region="bottom">It was really quick and easy to set up.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:23:00" end="00:08:27:00" region="bottom">And the benchmarks show that Drogon is one of the fastest available</p>
      <p begin="00:08:27:00" end="00:08:30:00" region="bottom"> C++ web frameworks.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:30:00" end="00:08:32:00" region="bottom">So yeah, if you need...</p>
      <p begin="00:08:32:00" end="00:08:35:00" region="bottom">I didn't know about this Drogon, and is it like header-only, perhaps,</p>
      <p begin="00:08:35:00" end="00:08:37:00" region="bottom"> thing, or is it?</p>
      <p begin="00:08:37:00" end="00:08:39:00" region="bottom">Let's see, I don't think it's header only.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:40:00" end="00:08:44:00" region="bottom">I think I had to build it and set it up</p>
      <p begin="00:08:44:00" end="00:08:47:00" region="bottom"> as a library dependency.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:47:00" end="00:08:49:00" region="bottom">That's actually good.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:49:00" end="00:08:53:00" region="bottom">If you want to increase build times, use header only libraries.</p>
      <p begin="00:08:53:00" end="00:08:56:00" region="bottom">In fact, you know, I wanted to ask you, what was the biggest bang for</p>
      <p begin="00:08:56:00" end="00:08:59:00" region="bottom"> your buck when you wanted to reduce the time for compilation,</p>
      <p begin="00:08:59:00" end="00:09:03:00" region="bottom"> did you find that too many includes or perhaps too</p>
      <p begin="00:09:03:00" end="00:09:06:00" region="bottom"> many templates were the problems or a combination?</p>
      <p begin="00:09:06:00" end="00:09:09:00" region="bottom">Circular dependencies were the</p>
      <p begin="00:09:09:00" end="00:09:12:00" region="bottom"> biggest culprit for long build times.</p>
      <p begin="00:09:12:00" end="00:09:16:00" region="bottom">I found an interesting thing about ccache.</p>
      <p begin="00:09:16:00" end="00:09:19:00" region="bottom">Apparently, the new version of ccache starting from version 4</p>
      <p begin="00:09:20:00" end="00:09:23:00" region="bottom"> supports a remote</p>
      <p begin="00:09:23:00" end="00:09:26:00" region="bottom"> cache in the same format as Bazel.</p>
      <p begin="00:09:26:00" end="00:09:29:00" region="bottom">So if you have a Bazel cache</p>
      <p begin="00:09:29:00" end="00:09:31:00" region="bottom"> server running somewhere,</p>
      <p begin="00:09:31:00" end="00:09:34:00" region="bottom">you can use it alongside Bazel</p>
      <p begin="00:09:34:00" end="00:09:37:00" region="bottom"> for any ccache remote storage.</p>
